---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';

let lit: any = null;
try {
  lit = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.resolve('public/data/literature.json'), 'utf8'));
} catch { /* renders its empty state */ }

const sections = lit?.sections ?? [];
const withPapers = sections.filter((s: any) => s.papers?.length);
---

<Base
  title="Reading"
  description="The literature behind what this archive computes: volatility surfaces, variance risk premia, momentum, and the multiple-testing methodology the celestial study rests on."
>
  <h1>Reading</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every method on this site came from somewhere. This is where — assembled
    from OpenAlex, grouped by the part of the pipeline each body of work
    supports, and ordered by relevance rather than by citation count.
  </p>

  {lit ? (
    <>
      <p class="meta">
        <span>{lit.n_papers} papers</span>
        <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>{withPapers.length} topics</span>
        <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>source <a href={lit.source_url}>{lit.source}</a></span>
      </p>

      <nav class="jump" aria-label="Topics">
        {withPapers.map((s: any) => (
          <a href={`#${s.key}`}>{s.name}</a>
        ))}
      </nav>

      {withPapers.map((s: any) => (
        <section class="topic" id={s.key}>
          <h2>{s.name}</h2>
          <p class="why">{s.why}</p>
          <ol class="papers">
            {s.papers.map((p: any) => (
              <li>
                <details>
                  <summary>
                    <span class="title">{p.title}</span>
                    <span class="byline">
                      {p.authors?.length ? p.authors.join(', ') : 'unattributed'}
                      {p.authors?.length >= 4 && ' et al.'}
                      {p.year && <span class="year num">{p.year}</span>}
                      {p.venue && <span class="venue">{p.venue}</span>}
                    </span>
                    <span class="marks">
                      {p.cited_by > 0 && (
                        <span class="num">cited {p.cited_by.toLocaleString()}</span>
                      )}
                      {p.open_access && <span class="oa">open access</span>}
                      {p.note && <span class="hasnote">note</span>}
                    </span>
                  </summary>

                  <div class="body">
                    {p.note ? (
                      <>
                        <p class="note-label">
                          Relevance to options markets
                          <span class="generated">written from the abstract, not the paper</span>
                        </p>
                        <p class="note">{p.note}</p>
                      </>
                    ) : (
                      <p class="note-missing">
                        No note yet. These are generated in batch; run
                        <span class="num"> make notes</span>.
                      </p>
                    )}

                    {p.excerpt && (
                      <p class="excerpt">
                        <span class="excerpt-label">From the abstract</span>
                        {p.excerpt}
                      </p>
                    )}

                    {p.url && (
                      <p class="source">
                        <a href={p.url}>Read the paper</a>
                        {p.doi && <span class="doi num">{p.doi.replace('https://doi.org/', 'doi:')}</span>}
                      </p>
                    )}
                  </div>
                </details>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </section>
      ))}

      <h2>On the notes</h2>
      <p>
        Each expanded entry carries a short note on how the work bears on
        options markets. Those notes are written by Claude
        {lit.note_model && <span class="num"> ({lit.note_model})</span>} from
        the paper's <em>abstract</em> — not from the paper.
      </p>
      <p>
        That distinction is the whole caveat. An abstract is a few hundred words
        the authors wrote to advertise the work. A note derived from it can say
        what the paper appears to be about and how that might bear on option
        pricing. It cannot tell you whether the method holds up, what the data
        actually showed, or whether the conclusion survived contact with
        referees. Every entry links to the paper so you can go and check, and
        the honest use of this page is as an index that tells you what to read
        next — not as a substitute for reading it.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        {lit.n_notes ?? 0} of {lit.n_papers} papers carry a note. Full abstracts
        are not republished: OpenAlex distributes them as an inverted index
        precisely so the text is not redistributed, and the excerpt shown is for
        identification.
      </p>

      <h2>On the sourcing</h2>
      <p>
        Not Google Scholar. It has no public API, its terms forbid programmatic
        access, and it blocks automated requests within a handful of calls — a
        page built on it would break quietly and deserve to. OpenAlex is an open
        catalogue of some 250 million works, CC0 licensed, and built to be
        queried.
      </p>
      <p>
        Results are ordered by relevance, never by citation count. Sorting a
        loose full-text match by citations returns the most-cited papers that
        happen to match on any word: a query for
        <em> implied volatility surface</em> ranked that way came back with
        papers on plastics chemistry and polymer degradation. Relevance
        ordering returns Gatheral.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        {lit.licence} Excerpts are truncated for identification; follow the link
        for the paper itself. A work appearing under more than one topic is
        listed under the first, which is ordered by how central it is here.
      </p>
    </>
  ) : (
    <div class="status">
      <h2>Not yet built</h2>
      <p>Run <span class="num">make reading</span> to assemble the list.</p>
    </div>
  )}
</Base>

<style>
  .jump {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem 1.3rem;
    padding: 0.9rem 0;
    margin: 1.4rem 0 0.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: 0.88rem;
  }

  .topic { margin: 2.6rem 0 0; }
  .why {
    color: var(--type-muted);
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.2rem;
  }

  .papers { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; counter-reset: paper; }
  .papers li {
    max-width: none;
    padding: 0.9rem 0 1rem 2.2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    position: relative;
    counter-increment: paper;
  }
  .papers li::before {
    content: counter(paper);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 1rem;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.76rem;
    color: var(--type-muted);
  }

  details summary {
    list-style: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    display: block;
  }
  details summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  details summary::before {
    content: '+';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0.9rem;
    top: 0.95rem;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    color: var(--type-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  details[open] summary::before { content: '−'; color: var(--amber); }
  details summary:hover .title { color: var(--amber); }

  .title {
    display: block;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .byline {
    display: block;
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--type-muted);
  }

  .body {
    margin: 0.9rem 0 0.2rem;
    padding-left: 0.9rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .note-label {
    margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--amber);
    max-width: none;
  }
  .note-label .generated {
    color: var(--type-muted);
    font-style: italic;
    margin-left: 0.6rem;
  }
  .note { margin: 0 0 0.9rem; font-size: 0.94rem; max-width: 74ch; }
  .note-missing {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--type-muted);
    max-width: none;
  }
  .excerpt-label {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--type-muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
  }
  .source { margin: 0.9rem 0 0; max-width: none; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .source .doi { margin-left: 0.9rem; color: var(--type-muted); font-size: 0.8rem; }
  .marks .hasnote { color: var(--amber); }
  .byline .year, .byline .venue { margin-left: 0.8rem; }
  .byline .venue { font-style: italic; }

  .excerpt {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--type-muted);
    max-width: 78ch;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .marks {
    display: flex;
    margin: 0.45rem 0 0;
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--type-muted);
    max-width: none;
  }
  .marks .oa { color: var(--amber); }

  .status {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--amber);
    background: var(--panel);
    padding: 1.1rem 1.3rem;
    margin: 1.6rem 0;
  }
  .status h2 { margin: 0 0 0.4rem; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--amber); }
</style>
